},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}, & 0<x<1 \\\\ \\frac{3(x-1)}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186484276 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2},$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{4{x}^{3}},\\, & 1<x<3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186484298 "The continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x)$ stated below. Given that $\\text{E}[X]=4$ compute $\\text{Var}[X].$ \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{27}, & 0<x<3, \\\\ \\frac{9-x}{27}, & 3\\le x<9, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486383 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{1}{XY}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}{y}^{3},\\, & 0<x<1,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486393 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=140$ from a population where $60\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,140.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{140}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [17592186486412 "Find $\\text{E}[X+3{Y}^{2}],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{15},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{x}{3}\\le y\\le 3; \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486430 "Find $\\text{E}[XY],$ given that random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3xy},\\, & 1\\le x\\le y,\\,e\\le y\\le {e}^{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186486435 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=66$ from a population where $42\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,66.$ Find an approximate sampling distribution of the statistic $$\n\\displaystyle X={\\sum }_{i=1}^{66}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [17592186491208 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}cx{y}^{2},\\, & 0\\le y\\le 1,\\,y\\le x\\le 2-y, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186496243 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What are the modes of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{3\\pi +2}, & 0\\le x\\le 3\\pi  \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186496247 "![](images/5c/5c6e4deccce395f5bdc8aadef8cd2c712804235c340ccae174f2067211a5a49c.png)\n\nConsider the region $R$ shown above. Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}, & (x,y)\\in S, \\\\ {x}^{2}+y, & (x,y)\\in T, \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in U.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186496248 "Evaluate the double integral $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1, \\\\ 2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y},\\, & \\text{if}\\,0<x<1,\\,1<y<2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand  $R$ is the domain of $f.$"] [17592186496258 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186496269 "Let $X$ be a random variable with the probability density function shown below. What is the mode of $X?$\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle f(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{24}x,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 7 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186498492 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\, & \\text{if}\\,-1<x<1,\\,0<y<2, \\\\ {2}^{xy},\\, & \\text{if}\\,1<x<3,\\,0<y<2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n{∬}_{(-1,3)\\times (0,2)}f(x,y)\\text{ }\\text{d}A=\n$$"] [17592186499451 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{40}$ be a random sample of size $n=40$ from a population with probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & 0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}=0.08,$ calculate $P(0.51<\\overset{―}{X}<0.55).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.* \n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5793\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5832\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5871\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6179\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6217\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.6255\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr